@model PairPageModel
@*
    The statement line: how many books quote this pair, and the worst thing that is true about the
    page's freshness right now.

    It is a partial for the same reason _PairTable is one — the live stream redraws it. A page whose
    table has just been replaced with figures from a healthy feed, under a headline still saying
    "one feed has stopped meaning anything", would be the loudest sentence on the surface left
    stale. If it is derived from the data, it moves with the data.

    THE SENTENCE ITSELF IS IN Models/Statement.cs, and it is re-derived every second by
    studio-ages.js from the same data-at / data-win attributes it advances the ages from. That is the
    fix for the worst thing this page did: the verdict was computed once, on the server, and never
    advanced, so a tab left open for three minutes showed "Every call is inside its window." in the
    largest type on the page over cells that had all flipped to "degraded". Only the SSE stream
    re-rendered it, and the stream is behind a button nobody has to press.

    The alternative was to stop the sentence claiming to be current — to caveat it, "as of the
    render". Rejected: with scripts ON that only explains the contradiction instead of removing it,
    because the ages under the sentence keep ticking and the reader can see them disagree with it;
    and with scripts OFF the caveat is redundant, since nothing on the page moves and the header's
    RENDERED stamp already dates the whole document. Re-deriving on the client is honest in both
    worlds: with no script the sentence, the ages and the fades are all as of the render together,
    and with a script they advance together. The one thing that must never happen — the sentence
    standing still while everything around it moves — cannot happen either way.
*@
@{
    var pair = $"{Model.BaseFamily}/{Model.QuoteFamily}";

    @* Three different numbers, counted where the pair list counts them. Rows are LISTINGS; venues
       are order books; platforms are exchanges. The page used to print the row count as all three,
       which read as four exchanges quoting the pair when one of them was in the table twice. *@
    var venues = Statement.Venues(Model.Rows);
    var platforms = Statement.Platforms(Model.Rows);
    var listings = Statement.Listings(Model.Rows);
    var perps = Statement.Perps(Model.Rows);
}
<div class="a-statement-row" data-live-region="statement">
    <h1 class="a-statement">
        @venues venue@(venues == 1 ? "" : "s") quote@(venues == 1 ? "s" : "") @pair.<em data-statement-verdict>@Statement.Verdict(Model.Rows)</em>
    </h1>
    <span class="csx-eyebrow" style="color:var(--text-faint)">
        @platforms platform@(platforms == 1 ? "" : "s") · @venues venue@(venues == 1 ? "" : "s") ·
        @listings listing@(listings == 1 ? "" : "s") · @perps perp · @(listings - perps) spot ·
        17 fields · ages live
    </span>
</div>
